Level 3: Conditional Statements

Mission: Write a script that checks if a file named hero.txt exists in the Arena directory. If it does, print Hero found!; otherwise, print Hero missing!.

This level requires us to check the Arena directory, use an if statement to see if the hero.txt file exist and then print out a statement depending on that. 


bench:~/DevOps/BattleBashArena/Arena# touch hero_checker.sh
bench:~/DevOps/BattleBashArena/Arena# vi hero_checker.sh 
bench:~/DevOps/BattleBashArena/Arena# ls
archer.txt           hero_checker.sh      mage.txt             number_generator.sh  warrior.txt
bench:~/DevOps/BattleBashArena/Arena# chmod +x hero_checker.sh 

To solve this level we can use the find command which will search for files, along with that we use an if statement to see if hero.txt is found. Initially I compared the output of the find command to hero.txt 
in the if statement but that did not work as find did not output the actual file it was looking for. 
    #!/bin/bash
    set -x
    check=find hero.txt
    
    if [ $check == hero.txt ]
    then
            echo "Hero found!"
    else
            echo "Hero missing!"
    fi


    bench:~/DevOps/BattleBashArena/Arena# ./hero_checker.sh 
    + check=find
    + hero.txt
    ./hero_checker.sh: line 3: hero.txt: command not found
    + '[' == hero.txt ']'
    ./hero_checker.sh: line 5: [: ==: unary operator expected
    + echo 'Hero missing!'
    Hero missing!

Using the set -x command I can see the value of the lines before they are executed.
+ '[' == hero.txt ']' shows us that the check value is not storing anything meaning the issue is with storing the file name into the $check variable.

I double checked this with a file already in the directory.
    bench:~/DevOps/BattleBashArena/Arena# ./hero_checker.sh 
    + check=find
    + mage.txt
    ./hero_checker.sh: line 3: mage.txt: command not found
    + '[' == mage.txt ']'
    ./hero_checker.sh: line 5: [: ==: unary operator expected
    + echo 'Hero missing!'
    Hero missing!
I then changed this by adding a $ on the actual find command itself.
    #!/bin/bash
    set -x
    check=$(find mage.txt)
    
    if [ $check == mage.txt ]
    then
            echo "Hero found!"
    else
            echo "Hero missing!"
    fi

And after testing with the mage.txt file that already exists.

bench:~/DevOps/BattleBashArena/Arena# ./hero_checker.sh 
++ find mage.txt
+ check=mage.txt
+ '[' mage.txt == mage.txt ']'
+ echo 'Hero found!'
Hero found!

We can see that the value of $check has the output of find stored in it.
